{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01GJwFJtEbRgmFadFaCBjX9Q","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I have now read the full paper (11 pages) and the review context YAML. Let me now dispatch two reviewers in parallel:\n\n- R1: Methods + Experiments\n- R2: Novelty + Related Work\n\nI'll use two agents running in parallel for Step 1 (independent reviews), then do Steps 2-4 (crossover, convergence, synthesis) myself.\n\nLet me carefully summarize what I've read from the paper:\n\n**Title:** Weakly-Supervised Progress Estimation for LLM Agent Trajectories\n\n**Core idea:** A weak-supervision framework using 3 labeling functions (command repetition, file-diff delta, outcome back-propagation) to generate training labels for a GRU probe that classifies sliding windows of agent steps as \"progress\" or \"stagnation.\"\n \nNow I'm looking at the key performance metrics. The high-confidence subset achieves strong results at 0.906 macro-F1, substantially beating the baseline, though performance drops when including noisier labels from the full dataset. The confidence-weighted approach partially recovers this gap. On the gold standard test set, the probe performs competitively with other methods, and notably the approach generalizes reasonably well to unseen agents in a zero-shot setting.\n\nLooking at the ablations and design choices, the non-NLP features show a significant performance gap, confirming that natural language understanding is essential. The architecture itself has modest sensitivity to changes, while the individual logical forms each contribute meaningfully. The model reaches competitive performance with just a quarter of the training data, and the window size of 5 proves optimal with good robustness to variation. Contrastive learning provides modest gains, and early termination offers a useful efficiency-accuracy tradeoff.\n\nI'm checking whether the paper covers the planned experiments from the context, and it looks like most of the key ablations are included. All experiments seem covered, though some are relegated to the appendix or discussion rather than main text. Now I'm ready to send this out to the two reviewers.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":14848,"cache_read_input_tokens":40528,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":14848},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3eaa2ba6-aa45-4be4-a541-eab7cb4981f6","uuid":"c300a4a0-fc64-4dfa-a107-e697167deeaa"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01GJwFJtEbRgmFadFaCBjX9Q","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I've read the full paper and the experiment context. Dispatching two independent reviewers (R1: Methods+Experiments, R2: Novelty+Related Work) in parallel for Step 1."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":14848,"cache_read_input_tokens":40528,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":14848},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3eaa2ba6-aa45-4be4-a541-eab7cb4981f6","uuid":"1898040e-d234-461d-b077-d38660a43ed6"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01GJwFJtEbRgmFadFaCBjX9Q","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01JkEJZpUPNgEhfSMiVuwcMv","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a top ML venue review. Your focus is **Methods and Experiments**. Use a 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept. Target venue acceptance rate ~25%.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** Weakly-Supervised Progress Estimation for LLM Agent Trajectories\n\nThe paper presents a weak-supervision framework for detecting \"semantic stagnation\" in LLM agent trajectories—where agents take superficially diverse but collectively unproductive actions. The framework uses 3 labeling functions (LFs): command repetition detection, file-diff delta measurement, and outcome back-propagation. These produce noisy labels via majority vote with confidence scores (c=1,2,3). A lightweight GRU probe with attention (542K params) is trained on these labels to classify sliding windows of k=5 steps as progress or stagnation.\n\n## Key Results\n- **High-confidence (conf≥2, 15,401 windows):** 0.906 macro-F1, +6.2pp over Repeat-3 heuristic\n- **Full data (conf≥1, 54,809 windows):** 0.82 F1 (-8.5pp from conf≥2 due to label noise)\n- **Confidence-weighted training:** 0.84 F1 (recovers 2pp)\n- **Human gold set (400 windows, Fleiss κ=0.62):** probe 0.77, LF oracle 0.75, Repeat-3 0.72, LLM judge (Claude FS) 0.82\n- **Cross-agent transfer (SWE-agent→OpenHands):** zero-shot 0.73 (-17.5pp), 200-shot recovers to 0.82\n- **Non-NLP structural features only:** 0.72 (18.5pp gap → NL understanding necessary)\n- **Architecture ablation:** 3.5pp range across 4 architectures (GRU+Attn best at 0.906)\n- **LF hold-one-out:** each LF contributes 1.5-3.5pp, none redundant\n- **Data scaling:** probe surpasses Repeat-3 at ~25% data (~4K windows)\n- **Window size ablation:** k=5 optimal; k=3 (0.86), k=7 (0.89)\n- **SupCon auxiliary loss:** +1pp on conf≥2, +1.5pp on full data\n- **Early termination sim:** 22% token savings, 3.1% success drop at threshold 0.7; 8.7% intervention paradox\n- **LF temporal dynamics:** command rep is leading (1.5 steps), file-diff synchronous, outcome BP lagging (2 steps)\n- **MeanPool+LR baseline:** 0.86 F1 (4.5pp below GRU)\n\n## Experiment Design Reference\nThe paper was designed to include experiments for: full-data training, LF oracle baseline, LLM-as-judge baseline, mean-pooling+LR baseline, error analysis, cross-agent transfer, window size ablation, architecture ablation, LF hold-one-out, SupCon ablation, non-NLP baseline, data scaling, early termination simulation, interpretability analysis, human gold set, and LF temporal dynamics.\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nEvaluate the following aspects thoroughly. For each, provide specific evidence from the paper:\n\n1. **Methodological soundness:** Is the weak-supervision framework well-motivated and correctly implemented? Are there methodological flaws?\n\n2. **Experimental rigor:** Are experiments well-designed, controlled, and sufficient? Are baselines appropriate and fair? Are there missing experiments or controls?\n\n3. **Statistical validity:** Are the results statistically significant? Are confidence intervals reported? Is the gold set (N=400) large enough?\n\n4. **Reproducibility:** Is there enough detail to reproduce? Are hyperparameters specified?\n\n5. **Evaluation concerns:** Is there circular evaluation (training and testing on LF-generated labels)? How well does the paper address this?\n\n6. **Specific weaknesses in methods/experiments** (enumerate)\n\n7. **Specific strengths in methods/experiments** (enumerate)\n\n8. **Questions for authors** (up to 5)\n\n9. **Your preliminary score and confidence**\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Report in structured sections. ~800-1000 words.","model":"opus"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":14848,"cache_read_input_tokens":40528,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":14848},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3eaa2ba6-aa45-4be4-a541-eab7cb4981f6","uuid":"b11fd5f6-c036-43e7-b5c8-9b0ed215c443"}
