+1pp on conf≥2, +1.5pp on full data\n- **Early termination sim:** 22% token savings, 3.1% success drop at threshold 0.7; 8.7% intervention paradox\n- **LF temporal dynamics:** command rep is leading (1.5 steps), file-diff synchronous, outcome BP lagging (2 steps)\n- **MeanPool+LR baseline:** 0.86 F1 (4.5pp below GRU)\n\n## Experiment Design Reference\nThe paper was designed to include experiments for: full-data training, LF oracle baseline, LLM-as-judge baseline, mean-pooling+LR baseline, error analysis, cross-agent transfer, window size ablation, architecture ablation, LF hold-one-out, SupCon ablation, non-NLP baseline, data scaling, early termination simulation, interpretability analysis, human gold set, and LF temporal dynamics.\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nEvaluate the following aspects thoroughly. For each, provide specific evidence from the paper:\n\n1. **Methodological soundness:** Is the weak-supervision framework well-motivated and correctly implemented? Are there methodological flaws?\n\n2. **Experimental rigor:** Are experiments well-designed, controlled, and sufficient? Are baselines appropriate and fair? Are there missing experiments or controls?\n\n3. **Statistical validity:** Are the results statistically significant? Are confidence intervals reported? Is the gold set (N=400) large enough?\n\n4. **Reproducibility:** Is there enough detail to reproduce? Are hyperparameters specified?\n\n5. **Evaluation concerns:** Is there circular evaluation (training and testing on LF-generated labels)? How well does the paper address this?\n\n6. **Specific weaknesses in methods/experiments** (enumerate)\n\n7. **Specific strengths in methods/experiments** (enumerate)\n\n8. **Questions for authors** (up to 5)\n\n9. **Your preliminary score and confidence**\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Report in structured sections. ~800-1000 words.","uuid":"8e022106-13ec-49ea-9ffd-ae9c501d0a33","session_id":"3eaa2ba6-aa45-4be4-a541-eab7cb4981f6"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a top ML venue review. Your focus is **Methods and Experiments**. Use a 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept. Target venue acceptance rate ~25%.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** Weakly-Supervised Progress Estimation for LLM Agent Trajectories\n\nThe paper presents a weak-supervision framework for detecting \"semantic stagnation\" in LLM agent trajectories—where agents take superficially diverse but collectively unproductive actions. The framework uses 3 labeling functions (LFs): command repetition detection, file-diff delta measurement, and outcome back-propagation. These produce noisy labels via majority vote with confidence scores (c=1,2,3). A lightweight GRU probe with attention (542K params) is trained on these labels to classify sliding windows of k=5 steps as progress or stagnation.\n\n## Key Results\n- **High-confidence (conf≥2, 15,401 windows):** 0.906 macro-F1, +6.2pp over Repeat-3 heuristic\n- **Full data (conf≥1, 54,809 windows):** 0.82 F1 (-8.5pp from conf≥2 due to label noise)\n- **Confidence-weighted training:** 0.84 F1 (recovers 2pp)\n- **Human gold set (400 windows, Fleiss κ=0.62):** probe 0.77, LF oracle 0.75, Repeat-3 0.72, LLM judge (Claude FS) 0.82\n- **Cross-agent transfer (SWE-agent→OpenHands):** zero-shot 0.73 (-17.5pp), 200-shot recovers to 0.82\n- **Non-NLP structural features only:** 0.72 (18.5pp gap → NL understanding necessary)\n- **Architecture ablation:** 3.5pp range across 4 architectures (GRU+Attn best at 0.906)\n- **LF hold-one-out:** each LF contributes 1.5-3.5pp, none redundant\n- **Data scaling:** probe surpasses Repeat-3 at ~25% data (~4K windows)\n- **Window size ablation:** k=5 optimal; k=3 (0.86), k=7 (0.89)\n- **SupCon auxiliary loss:** +1pp on conf≥2, +1.5pp on full data\n- **Early termination sim:** 22% token savings, 3.1% success drop at threshold 0.7; 8.7% intervention paradox\n- **LF temporal dynamics:** command rep is leading (1.5 steps), file-diff synchronous, outcome BP lagging (2 steps)\n- **MeanPool+LR baseline:** 0.86 F1 (4.5pp below GRU)\n\n## Experiment Design Reference\nThe paper was designed to include experiments for: full-data training, LF oracle baseline, LLM-as-judge baseline, mean-pooling+LR baseline, error analysis, cross-agent transfer, window size ablation, architecture ablation, LF hold-one-out, SupCon ablation, non-NLP baseline, data scaling, early termination simulation, interpretability analysis, human gold set, and LF temporal dynamics.\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nEvaluate the following aspects thoroughly. For each, provide specific evidence from the paper:\n\n1. **Methodological soundness:** Is the weak-supervision framework well-motivated and correctly implemented? Are there methodological flaws?\n\n2. **Experimental rigor:** Are experiments well-designed, controlled, and sufficient? Are baselines appropriate and fair? Are there missing experiments or controls?\n\n3. **Statistical validity:** Are the results statistically significant? Are confidence intervals reported? Is the gold set (N=400) large enough?\n\n4. **Reproducibility:** Is there enough detail to reproduce? Are hyperparameters specified?\n\n5. **Evaluation concerns:** Is there circular evaluation (training and testing on LF-generated labels)? How well does the paper address this?\n\n6. **Specific weaknesses in methods/experiments** (enumerate)\n\n7. **Specific strengths in methods/experiments** (enumerate)\n\n8. **Questions for authors** (up to 5)\n\n9. **Your preliminary score and confidence**\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Report in structured sections. ~800-1000 words."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01JkEJZpUPNgEhfSMiVuwcMv","session_id":"3eaa2ba6-aa45-4be4-a541-eab7cb4981f6","uuid":"0370a973-fe6f-455f-9d2e-d6636e12f31d","timestamp":"2026-05-14T08:58:49.991Z"}
