{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01GJwFJtEbRgmFadFaCBjX9Q","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01MbVMY3G7diYAUeDjF1aRAe","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a top ML venue review. Your focus is **Novelty and Related Work**. Use a 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept. Target venue acceptance rate ~25%.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** Weakly-Supervised Progress Estimation for LLM Agent Trajectories\n\nThe paper presents a weak-supervision framework for detecting \"semantic stagnation\" in LLM agent trajectories. Three labeling functions (command repetition, file-diff delta, outcome back-propagation) generate noisy labels via majority vote. A GRU probe (542K params) trained on these labels classifies sliding windows of 5 steps as progress/stagnation.\n\n**Three claimed contributions:**\n1. A weak-supervision framework transferring data programming to temporal, non-i.i.d. trajectory signals\n2. A systematic empirical study showing a GRU probe trained on weakly-supervised labels captures semantic stagnation patterns heuristics miss\n3. Evidence for cross-agent transferability with efficient few-shot adaptation\n\n## Key Results\n- conf≥2: 0.906 macro-F1 (+6.2pp over Repeat-3)\n- Full data: 0.82 F1 (-8.5pp due to noisy conf=1 labels)\n- Gold set (N=400, κ=0.62): probe 0.77 vs LLM judge 0.82 vs LF oracle 0.75 vs Repeat-3 0.72\n- Cross-agent: zero-shot 0.73, 200-shot recovers to 0.82\n- Architecture ablation: 3.5pp range across architectures\n- LF hold-one-out: each contributes 1.5-3.5pp\n- Non-NLP features: 0.72 (18.5pp below embedding-based)\n- Error analysis: 187 windows probe catches that Repeat-3 misses vs 34 reverse\n\n## Related Work as Presented\nThe paper positions against three lines:\n1. **Agent monitoring/error detection:** Cycle detectors (structural repetition only), step-level value estimators (AgentPRM, SWE-TRACE—no temporal modeling across windows), self-reflection (agent evaluates itself but may be unreliable when stagnating)\n2. **Weak supervision/data programming:** Snorkel paradigm extended from i.i.d. text to temporal trajectory signals\n3. **Progress estimation:** Goal-conditioned RL value functions (require reward); this work is reward-free\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nEvaluate the following aspects thoroughly:\n\n1. **Novelty assessment:** How novel is each of the three contributions? Is the combination of data programming + trajectory monitoring genuinely new, or is it straightforward application of existing techniques (Snorkel + GRU)?\n\n2. **Related work coverage:** Are important baselines or related systems missing? Consider:\n   - AgentPRM, SWE-TRACE, and other step-level estimators\n   - Agent loop/cycle detection methods\n   - Process reward models (PRMs) for reasoning\n   - Self-reflection and self-correction in agents\n   - Snorkel, data programming, and programmatic labeling\n   - Trajectory-level evaluation and reward modeling\n\n3. **Positioning and differentiation:** Does the paper clearly differentiate from prior work? Are the claimed gaps in prior work real?\n\n4. **Significance and impact:** Would this framework meaningfully advance the field? Is the problem important enough? Who would use this?\n\n5. **Writing quality and presentation:** Is the paper well-written? Is the framing honest about limitations?\n\n6. **Specific weaknesses** (enumerate)\n\n7. **Specific strengths** (enumerate)\n\n8. **Questions for authors** (up to 5)\n\n9. **Your preliminary score and confidence**\n\nBe rigorous. Consider whether the \"framework as primary contribution\" framing is justified given that the components (LFs, majority vote, GRU probe) are individually straightforward. Report in structured sections. ~800-1000 words.","model":"opus"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":14848,"cache_read_input_tokens":40528,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":14848},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3eaa2ba6-aa45-4be4-a541-eab7cb4981f6","uuid":"8ecf1eba-e420-404d-b7b3-618a6cc26e40"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"ab7ae31956b48352a","tool_use_id":"toolu_01MbVMY3G7diYAUeDjF1aRAe","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a top ML venue review. Your focus is **Novelty and Related Work**. Use a 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept. Target venue acceptance rate ~25%.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** Weakly-Supervised Progress Estimation for LLM Agent Trajectories\n\nThe paper presents a weak-supervision framework for detecting \"semantic stagnation\" in LLM agent trajectories. Three labeling functions (command repetition, file-diff delta, outcome back-propagation) generate noisy labels via majority vote. A GRU probe (542K params) trained on these labels classifies sliding windows of 5 steps as progress/stagnation.\n\n**Three claimed contributions:**\n1. A weak-supervision framework transferring data programming to temporal, non-i.i.d. trajectory signals\n2. A systematic empirical study showing a GRU probe trained on weakly-supervised labels captures semantic stagnation patterns heuristics miss\n3. Evidence for cross-agent transferability with efficient few-shot adaptation\n\n## Key Results\n- conf≥2: 0.906 macro-F1 (+6.2pp over Repeat-3)\n- Full data: 0.82 F1 (-8.5pp due to noisy conf=1 labels)\n- Gold set (N=400, κ=0.62): probe 0.77 vs LLM judge 0.82 vs LF oracle 0.75 vs Repeat-3 0.72\n- Cross-agent: zero-shot 0.73, 200-shot recovers to 0.82\n- Architecture ablation: 3.5pp range across architectures\n- LF hold-one-out: each contributes 1.5-3.5pp\n- Non-NLP features: 0.72 (18.5pp below embedding-based)\n- Error analysis: 187 windows probe catches that Repeat-3 misses vs 34 reverse\n\n## Related Work as Presented\nThe paper positions against three lines:\n1. **Agent monitoring/error detection:** Cycle detectors (structural repetition only), step-level value estimators (AgentPRM, SWE-TRACE—no temporal modeling across windows), self-reflection (agent evaluates itself but may be unreliable when stagnating)\n2. **Weak supervision/data programming:** Snorkel paradigm extended from i.i.d. text to temporal trajectory signals\n3. **Progress estimation:** Goal-conditioned RL value functions (require reward); this work is reward-free\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nEvaluate the following aspects thoroughly:\n\n1. **Novelty assessment:** How novel is each of the three contributions? Is the combination of data programming + trajectory monitoring genuinely new, or is it straightforward application of existing techniques (Snorkel + GRU)?\n\n2. **Related work coverage:** Are important baselines or related systems missing? Consider:\n   - AgentPRM, SWE-TRACE, and other step-level estimators\n   - Agent loop/cycle detection methods\n   - Process reward models (PRMs) for reasoning\n   - Self-reflection and self-correction in agents\n   - Snorkel, data programming, and programmatic labeling\n   - Trajectory-level evaluation and reward modeling\n\n3. **Positioning and differentiation:** Does the paper clearly differentiate from prior work? Are the claimed gaps in prior work real?\n\n4. **Significance and impact:** Would this framework meaningfully advance the field? Is the problem important enough? Who would use this?\n\n5. **Writing quality and presentation:** Is the paper well-written? Is the framing honest about limitations?\n\n6. **Specific weaknesses** (enumerate)\n\n7. **Specific strengths** (enumerate)\n\n8. **Questions for authors** (up to 5)\n\n9. **Your preliminary score and confidence**\n\nBe rigorous. Consider whether the \"framework as primary contribution\" framing is justified given that the components (LFs, majority vote, GRU probe) are individually straightforward. Report in structured sections. ~800-1000 words.","uuid":"58536694-831b-4dd2-b142-18d85674f396","session_id":"3eaa2ba6-aa45-4be4-a541-eab7cb4981f6"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a top ML venue review. Your focus is **Novelty and Related Work**. Use a 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept. Target venue acceptance rate ~25%.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** Weakly-Supervised Progress Estimation for LLM Agent Trajectories\n\nThe paper presents a weak-supervision framework for detecting \"semantic stagnation\" in LLM agent trajectories. Three labeling functions (command repetition, file-diff delta, outcome back-propagation) generate noisy labels via majority vote. A GRU probe (542K params) trained on these labels classifies sliding windows of 5 steps as progress/stagnation.\n\n**Three claimed contributions:**\n1. A weak-supervision framework transferring data programming to temporal, non-i.i.d. trajectory signals\n2. A systematic empirical study showing a GRU probe trained on weakly-supervised labels captures semantic stagnation patterns heuristics miss\n3. Evidence for cross-agent transferability with efficient few-shot adaptation\n\n## Key Results\n- conf≥2: 0.906 macro-F1 (+6.2pp over Repeat-3)\n- Full data: 0.82 F1 (-8.5pp due to noisy conf=1 labels)\n- Gold set (N=400, κ=0.62): probe 0.77 vs LLM judge 0.82 vs LF oracle 0.75 vs Repeat-3 0.72\n- Cross-agent: zero-shot 0.73, 200-shot recovers to 0.82\n- Architecture ablation: 3.5pp range across architectures\n- LF hold-one-out: each contributes 1.5-3.5pp\n- Non-NLP features: 0.72 (18.5pp below embedding-based)\n- Error analysis: 187 windows probe catches that Repeat-3 misses vs 34 reverse\n\n## Related Work as Presented\nThe paper positions against three lines:\n1. **Agent monitoring/error detection:** Cycle detectors (structural repetition only), step-level value estimators (AgentPRM, SWE-TRACE—no temporal modeling across windows), self-reflection (agent evaluates itself but may be unreliable when stagnating)\n2. **Weak supervision/data programming:** Snorkel paradigm extended from i.i.d. text to temporal trajectory signals\n3. **Progress estimation:** Goal-conditioned RL value functions (require reward); this work is reward-free\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nEvaluate the following aspects thoroughly:\n\n1. **Novelty assessment:** How novel is each of the three contributions? Is the combination of data programming + trajectory monitoring genuinely new, or is it straightforward application of existing techniques (Snorkel + GRU)?\n\n2. **Related work coverage:** Are important baselines or related systems missing? Consider:\n   - AgentPRM, SWE-TRACE, and other step-level estimators\n   - Agent loop/cycle detection methods\n   - Process reward models (PRMs) for reasoning\n   - Self-reflection and self-correction in agents\n   - Snorkel, data programming, and programmatic labeling\n   - Trajectory-level evaluation and reward modeling\n\n3. **Positioning and differentiation:** Does the paper clearly differentiate from prior work? Are the claimed gaps in prior work real?\n\n4. **Significance and impact:** Would this framework meaningfully advance the field? Is the problem important enough? Who would use this?\n\n5. **Writing quality and presentation:** Is the paper well-written? Is the framing honest about limitations?\n\n6. **Specific weaknesses** (enumerate)\n\n7. **Specific strengths** (enumerate)\n\n8. **Questions for authors** (up to 5)\n\n9. **Your preliminary score and confidence**\n\nBe rigorous. Consider whether the \"framework as primary contribution\" framing is justified given that the components (LFs, majority vote, GRU probe) are individually straightforward. Report in structured sections. ~800-1000 words."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01MbVMY3G7diYAUeDjF1aRAe","session_id":"3eaa2ba6-aa45-4be4-a541-eab7cb4981f6","uuid":"14c58fa8-1521-447b-9aa5-fb2956235025","timestamp":"2026-05-14T08:59:09.502Z"}
