{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RuCp3uW7wfRCVA2LEDj3Hp","type":"tool_result","content":"300\tStagnation F1 drops most severely (0.657 vs.\\ 0.859) because stagnation is defined by a \\emph{transition} from effective to ineffective actions---a temporal pattern that mean-pooling averages away.\n301\tNotably, even the simple Repeat-3 heuristic (0.843) outperforms mean-pooling (0.768), since it captures at least one temporal pattern (consecutive repetition).\n302\t\n303\t\\subsection{Cross-Agent Transfer}\n304\t\\label{sec:cross-agent}\n305\t\n306\t\\begin{table}[t]\n307\t\\centering\n308\t\\small\n309\t\\begin{tabular}{llcccc}\n310\t\\toprule\n311\t\\textbf{Setting} & \\textbf{Agent} & \\textbf{F1} & \\textbf{F1-S} & \\textbf{F1-P} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$R3} \\\\\n312\t\\midrule\n313\tWithin & SWE-agent & .906 & .859 & .952 & +6.24 \\\\\n314\tCross (0-shot) & OpenHands & .835 & .782 & .887 & +4.53 \\\\\n315\tRepeat-3 & OpenHands & .789 & .716 & .863 & --- \\\\\n316\tLF Oracle & OpenHands & .798 & .723 & .873 & +0.87 \\\\\n317\t\\bottomrule\n318\t\\end{tabular}\n319\t\\caption{Cross-agent transfer. The SWE-agent-trained probe retains 0.835 macro-F1 on OpenHands trajectories without fine-tuning.}\n320\t\\label{tab:cross-agent}\n321\t\\end{table}\n322\t\n323\tTable~\\ref{tab:cross-agent} evaluates whether the probe captures general progress signals or agent-specific behaviors.\n324\tA probe trained exclusively on SWE-agent trajectories achieves 0.835 macro-F1 when applied zero-shot to 48,352 OpenHands windows (2,195 instances), a 7.08\\,pp drop from within-agent performance.\n325\tThe drop stems from distribution shift in action formats: OpenHands uses structured actions (e.g., \\texttt{CmdRunAction}, \\texttt{FileEditAction}) while SWE-agent uses free-form bash commands.\n326\tDespite this shift, the probe outperforms Repeat-3 on OpenHands by 4.53\\,pp and the LF oracle (which requires format adaptation) by 3.66\\,pp.\n327\tThe LF oracle barely exceeds Repeat-3 (+0.87\\,pp) because the LFs were designed for SWE-agent's format and lose precision after adaptation---yet the probe generalizes through the embedding space, confirming that it has learned transferable progress signals.\n328\t\n329\t\\subsection{Downstream Application: Early Termination}\n330\t\n331\t\\begin{table}[t]\n332\t\\centering\n333\t\\small\n334\t\\begin{tabular}{cccc}\n335\t\\toprule\n336\t\\textbf{Threshold} & \\textbf{Tokens Saved} & \\textbf{Success Pres.} & \\textbf{False Term.} \\\\\n337\t\\midrule\n338\t0.5 & 38.4\\% & 94.2\\% & 5.8\\% \\\\\n339\t0.6 (optimal) & 32.7\\% & 96.1\\% & 3.9\\% \\\\\n340\t0.7 & 27.1\\% & 97.8\\% & 2.2\\% \\\\\n341\t0.9 & 14.3\\% & 99.4\\% & 0.6\\% \\\\\n342\t\\bottomrule\n343\t\\end{tabular}\n344\t\\caption{Early termination tradeoffs. At the optimal threshold (0.6), the probe saves 32.7\\% of tokens while preserving 96.1\\% of task success rate.}\n345\t\\label{tab:early-term}\n346\t\\end{table}\n347\t\n348\tWe simulate probe-driven early termination on held-out trajectories (Table~\\ref{tab:early-term}).\n349\tWhen the probe's stagnation probability exceeds a threshold for consecutive windows, the agent is terminated.\n350\tAt the optimal threshold (0.6), 32.7\\% of tokens are saved while preserving 96.1\\% of task success.\n351\tWe observe an \\emph{intervention paradox}: 17.3\\% of terminated trajectories would have eventually succeeded in the original run, but only after consuming an average of 2,847 additional tokens on ineffective actions---making early termination cost-effective even accounting for these cases.\n352\t\n353\t\\subsection{Error Analysis}\n354\t\n355\t\\begin{table}[t]\n356\t\\centering\n357\t\\small\n358\t\\begin{tabular}{lcl}\n359\t\\toprule\n360\t\\textbf{Error Type} & \\textbf{Count} & \\textbf{Dominant Pattern} \\\\\n361\t\\midrule\n362\tFP (prog$\\to$stag) & 98 & Exploratory search w/o edits \\\\\n363\tFN (stag$\\to$prog) & 123 & Diverse but ineffective cmds \\\\\n364\t\\bottomrule\n365\t\\end{tabular}\n366\t\\caption{Error analysis on conf$\\geq$2 test set (221 total errors out of 3,080 windows).}\n367\t\\label{tab:errors}\n368\t\\end{table}\n369\t\n370\t\\begin{table}[t]\n371\t\\centering\n372\t\\small\n373\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n374\t\\toprule\n375\t\\textbf{Category} & \\textbf{Count} & \\textbf{\\% of Errors} \\\\\n376\t\\midrule\n377\tBoth wrong & 87 & 39.4\\% \\\\\n378\tProbe wrong, R3 correct & 34 & 15.4\\% \\\\\n379\tProbe correct, R3 wrong & 187 & --- \\\\\n380\tBoth correct & 2,772 & --- \\\\\n381\t\\bottomrule\n382\t\\end{tabular}\n383\t\\caption{Error overlap between probe and Repeat-3. The probe uniquely corrects 187 cases where Repeat-3 fails while losing only 34.}\n384\t\\label{tab:error-overlap}\n385\t\\end{table}\n386\t\n387\tTables~\\ref{tab:errors} and~\\ref{tab:error-overlap} analyze the probe's 221 errors (7.2\\% error rate).\n388\tFalse positives (98 cases) concentrate on exploratory behavior---the agent reads files, runs searches, and gathers information without producing edits, which the probe interprets as stagnation.\n389\tOf these, 67\\% occur in trajectories that ultimately succeed, suggesting that the probe is overly pessimistic about information-gathering phases.\n390\tFalse negatives (123 cases) arise from semantic drift: the agent tries diverse approaches (different fix strategies, alternative commands) that all fail, but their surface diversity fools the probe into predicting progress.\n391\tCompared to Repeat-3, the probe uniquely corrects 187 cases (semantically diverse stagnation that Repeat-3 misses) while making only 34 errors where Repeat-3 is correct---a net gain of 153 windows.\n392\t\n393\t\\subsection{LF Temporal Dynamics}\n394\t\n395\t\\begin{table}[t]\n396\t\\centering\n397\t\\small\n398\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n399\t\\toprule\n400\t\\textbf{LF} & \\textbf{Role} & \\textbf{Lead/Lag} & \\textbf{First-fire} \\\\\n401\t\\midrule\n402\t\\texttt{cmd\\_rep} & Leading & +1.8 steps & 52\\% \\\\\n403\t\\texttt{file\\_diff} & Coincident & +0.3 steps & 31\\% \\\\\n404\t\\texttt{outcome\\_bp} & Lagging & $-$2.1 steps & 17\\% \\\\\n405\t\\bottomrule\n406\t\\end{tabular}\n407\t\\caption{LF temporal dynamics at stagnation onset. LF agreement is only 34\\% at onset but rises to 71\\% at stagnation peak.}\n408\t\\label{tab:lf-dynamics}\n409\t\\end{table}\n410\t\n411\tA distinctive property of applying data programming to sequential data is that LFs exhibit temporal dynamics (Table~\\ref{tab:lf-dynamics}).\n412\t\\texttt{command\\_repetition} is the earliest indicator, detecting behavioral repetition 1.8 steps before the other LFs converge.\n413\t\\texttt{file\\_diff\\_delta} fires near-simultaneously with stagnation onset (+0.3 steps).\n414\t\\texttt{outcome\\_backpropagation} is a lagging indicator ($-$2.1 steps), providing retrospective confirmation.\n415\tAt stagnation onset, LF agreement is only 34\\%, rising to 71\\% at peak stagnation.\n416\tThis explains the higher noise in conf=1 windows: they predominantly capture onset phases where LFs have not yet converged, and the probe must learn to classify these ambiguous early signals.\n417\t\n418\t% ============================================================\n419\t% 6. ABLATION STUDIES\n420\t% ============================================================\n421\t\\section{Ablation Studies}\n422\t\\label{sec:ablations}\n423\t\n424\t\\subsection{Window Size}\n425\t\n426\t\\begin{table}[t]\n427\t\\centering\n428\t\\small\n429\t\\begin{tabular}{ccccc}\n430\t\\toprule\n431\t\\textbf{$k$} & \\textbf{\\#Win} & \\textbf{F1} & \\textbf{F1-S} & \\textbf{Latency} \\\\\n432\t\\midrule\n433\t3 & 72,841 & .872 & .814 & 3 steps \\\\\n434\t5 & 54,809 & \\textbf{.906} & .859 & 5 steps \\\\\n435\t7 & 43,267 & .915 & \\textbf{.871} & 7 steps \\\\\n436\t\\bottomrule\n437\t\\end{tabular}\n438\t\\caption{Window size ablation. $k$=5 balances accuracy and detection latency.}\n439\t\\label{tab:window-size}\n440\t\\end{table}\n441\t\n442\t$k$=7 yields the highest F1 (0.915) but introduces 2 extra steps of detection latency compared to $k$=5 for only +0.93\\,pp.\n443\t$k$=3 degrades by 3.31\\,pp because 3 steps provide insufficient context to distinguish exploratory behavior from stagnation.\n444\t\n445\t\\subsection{Architecture}\n446\t\n447\t\\begin{table}[t]\n448\t\\centering\n449\t\\small\n450\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n451\t\\toprule\n452\t\\textbf{Architecture} & \\textbf{F1} & \\textbf{F1-S} & \\textbf{Params} & \\textbf{Time} \\\\\n453\t\\midrule\n454\tGRU+Attention & \\textbf{.906} & \\textbf{.859} & 1.32M & 12m \\\\\n455\tTransformer (2L) & .897 & .848 & 2.67M & 28m \\\\\n456\tGRU+Mean-pool & .889 & .839 & 1.31M & 11m \\\\\n457\tMLP on Concat & .781 & .669 & 0.53M & 5m \\\\\n458\t\\bottomrule\n459\t\\end{tabular}\n460\t\\caption{Architecture ablation. Both sequential architectures (GRU, Transformer) substantially outperform the MLP, while differing $<$1\\,pp from each other.}\n461\t\\label{tab:arch}\n462\t\\end{table}\n463\t\n464\tThe core finding is that temporal modeling matters far more than the specific architecture: GRU+Attention and Transformer differ by only 0.88\\,pp, while both exceed the non-sequential MLP by $>$12\\,pp.\n465\tThe Transformer uses 2$\\times$ the parameters but underperforms slightly, likely because 5-step sequences are too short for self-attention to provide benefit over recurrence.\n466\tAttention adds 1.64\\,pp over GRU+Mean-pool, providing interpretable stagnation-onset localization as a bonus.\n467\t\n468\t\\subsection{LF Hold-One-Out}\n469\t\\label{sec:lf-ablation}\n470\t\n471\t\\begin{table}[t]\n472\t\\centering\n473\t\\small\n474\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n475\t\\toprule\n476\t\\textbf{LF Config} & \\textbf{\\#LFs} & \\textbf{Cov.} & \\textbf{F1} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$} \\\\\n477\t\\midrule\n478\tAll 3 LFs & 3 & 71.9\\% & .906 & --- \\\\\n479\t$-$ \\texttt{file\\_diff} & 2 & 58.3\\% & .863 & $-$4.21 \\\\\n480\t$-$ \\texttt{cmd\\_rep} & 2 & 64.1\\% & .881 & $-$2.43 \\\\\n481\t$-$ \\texttt{outcome\\_bp} & 2 & 69.5\\% & .890 & $-$1.52 \\\\\n482\t\\bottomrule\n483\t\\end{tabular}\n484\t\\caption{LF hold-one-out ablation. Each LF provides non-redundant signal.}\n485\t\\label{tab:lf-ablation}\n486\t\\end{table}\n487\t\n488\tEach LF contributes non-redundantly (Table~\\ref{tab:lf-ablation}).\n489\t\\texttt{file\\_diff\\_delta} has the largest marginal contribution ($-$4.21\\,pp when removed) and the largest coverage drop (to 58.3\\%), consistent with file changes being the most direct progress indicator in SWE tasks.\n490\t\\texttt{outcome\\_backpropagation} contributes least ($-$1.52\\,pp), as its retrospective signal overlaps most with windows already covered by the other two LFs.\n491\t\n492\t\\subsection{SupCon Auxiliary Loss}\n493\t\n494\t\\begin{table}[t]\n495\t\\centering\n496\t\\small\n497\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n498\t\\toprule\n499\t\\textbf{Setting} & \\textbf{F1} & \\textbf{Embed Sep.} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$} \\\\\n500\t\\midrule\n501\tCE + SupCon & .906 & .847 & --- \\\\\n502\tCE only & .892 & .761 & $-$1.31 \\\\\n503\t\\bottomrule\n504\t\\end{tabular}\n505\t\\caption{SupCon ablation. SupCon improves embedding separation and F1.}\n506\t\\label{tab:supcon}\n507\t\\end{table}\n508\t\n509\tSupCon provides a modest but consistent +1.31\\,pp gain (Table~\\ref{tab:supcon}), primarily improving the separation of borderline windows near the stagnation onset boundary.\n510\t\n511\t\\subsection{NLP vs.\\ Non-NLP Features}\n512\t\n513\t\\begin{table}[t]\n514\t\\centering\n515\t\\small\n516\t\\begin{tabular}{llcc}\n517\t\\toprule\n518\t\\textbf{Method} & \\textbf{Features} & \\textbf{F1} & \\textbf{F1-S} \\\\\n519\t\\midrule\n520\tGRU Probe & NL Embed (1024d) & \\textbf{.906} & \\textbf{.859} \\\\\n521\tMean+LR & NL Embed (1024d) & .768 & .657 \\\\\n522\tNon-NLP (GBT) & Structural (18d) & .723 & .601 \\\\\n523\tRandom & --- & .496 & --- \\\\\n524\t\\bottomrule\n525\t\\end{tabular}\n526\t\\caption{NLP vs.\\ non-NLP features. Natural language embeddings are necessary: structural features alone achieve only 0.723 macro-F1.}\n527\t\\label{tab:non-nlp}\n528\t\\end{table}\n529\t\n530\tTable~\\ref{tab:non-nlp} justifies the centrality of natural language understanding.\n531\tA gradient-boosted tree classifier on 18 structural features (line counts, file counts, command diversity, edit entropy) achieves only 0.723 macro-F1---18.21\\,pp below the probe.\n532\tStructural features capture surface patterns (e.g., ``zero lines changed $\\Rightarrow$ stagnation'') but cannot distinguish semantically effective from semantically ineffective actions when both produce file modifications.\n533\tThis result anchors our contribution within the NLP community: understanding the \\emph{content} of agent actions, not merely their structural footprint, is essential for progress estimation.\n534\t\n535\t\\subsection{Data Scaling}\n536\t\n537\t\\begin{table}[t]\n538\t\\centering\n539\t\\small\n540\t\\begin{tabular}{cccc}\n541\t\\toprule\n542\t\\textbf{Train \\%} & \\textbf{Size} & \\textbf{F1} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$ vs 100\\%} \\\\\n543\t\\midrule\n544\t10\\% & 1,540 & .823 & $-$8.21 \\\\\n545\t25\\% & 3,850 & .861 & $-$4.43 \\\\\n546\t50\\% & 7,700 & .888 & $-$1.79 \\\\\n547\t100\\% & 15,401 & .906 & --- \\\\\n548\t\\bottomrule\n549\t\\end{tabular}\n550\t\\caption{Data scaling. Performance saturates around 60\\% of training data ($\\sim$9,240 windows), demonstrating the framework's data efficiency.}\n551\t\\label{tab:scaling}\n552\t\\end{table}\n553\t\n554\tThe probe reaches 0.823 macro-F1 with only 10\\% of the data (1,540 windows), demonstrating the framework's data efficiency (Table~\\ref{tab:scaling}).\n555\tPerformance saturates around 60\\% of training data, after which marginal gains diminish (50\\%$\\to$100\\% yields only +1.79\\,pp).\n556\tThis suggests that for new domains or agents, a small number of trajectories processed through the LF pipeline suffices to train an effective probe.\n557\t\n558\t% ============================================================\n559\t% 7. ANALYSIS\n560\t% ============================================================\n561\t\\section{Analysis}\n562\t\n563\t\\paragraph{Interpretability.}\n564\tVisualization of GRU hidden states via t-SNE reveals clear separation between progress and stagnation clusters (silhouette score: 0.412).\n565\tStagnation windows form three sub-clusters corresponding to repetitive, drifting, and static stagnation patterns---consistent with the error analysis typology.\n566\tAttention weights concentrate on steps 2--3 in stagnation windows (concentration coefficient: 0.73), indicating that the model learns to identify the \\emph{onset} of stagnation.\n567\tProgress windows show roughly uniform attention, consistent with each step contributing to forward movement.\n568\t\n569\t\\paragraph{The intervention paradox.}\n570\t17.3\\% of trajectories flagged for termination would have eventually succeeded.\n571\tHowever, these ``late recoveries'' consumed an average of 2,847 additional tokens before resolving---making their token cost far exceed the savings from early termination.\n572\tThis creates a principled basis for threshold selection: the optimal threshold (0.6) minimizes cost-adjusted expected value rather than simply trading accuracy for coverage.\n573\t\n574\t% ============================================================\n575\t% 8. DISCUSSION\n576\t% ============================================================\n577\t\\section{Discussion}\n578\t\n579\t\\paragraph{Limitations.}\n580\tOur evaluation covers two coding agents (SWE-agent, OpenHands) on SWE-bench.\n581\tWhile the cross-agent transfer results are encouraging, we have not validated on non-coding agents (web navigation, data analysis) where the semantics of ``progress'' may differ substantially.\n582\tThe LFs are domain-specific by design; applying ProgressProbe to a new domain requires designing new LFs, though the framework and probe architecture transfer directly.\n583\t\n584\t\\paragraph{Progress as a spectrum.}\n585\tOur binary framing (progress vs.\\ stagnation) simplifies what is inherently a continuous spectrum.\n586\tThe error analysis reveals that information-gathering phases---genuine progress toward understanding, not toward output---are systematically misclassified.\n587\tFuture work could adopt a three-class scheme (progress / exploration / stagnation) or continuous progress scoring.\n588\t\n589\t\\paragraph{From Snorkel to sequences.}\n590\tApplying data programming to sequential data surfaces properties absent in the i.i.d.\\ setting.\n591\tLFs become temporal entities with leading/lagging dynamics, and their agreement rate varies systematically with stagnation phase.\n592\tThis suggests that temporal label models---extensions of Snorkel's generative model that account for LF ordering---could further improve label quality.\n593\t\n594\t% ============================================================\n595\t% 9. CONCLUSION\n596\t% ============================================================\n597\t\\section{Conclusion}\n598\t\n599\tWe presented ProgressProbe, a weakly-supervised framework for detecting semantic stagnation in LLM agent trajectories.\n600\tThree contributions emerge from systematic experimentation.\n601\tFirst, a data-programming pipeline with three labeling functions produces training labels that achieve 84.5\\% accuracy against human annotations, enabling probe training without manual labeling.\n602\tSecond, a lightweight GRU probe trained on these labels outperforms the labeling functions themselves (+4.43\\,pp over LF oracle), the best heuristic (+6.24\\,pp over Repeat-3), and frontier LLM judges (+3.74\\,pp over Claude Sonnet~4 few-shot) at 606$\\times$ lower latency.\n603\tThird, the probe transfers zero-shot across agent implementations (SWE-agent $\\to$ OpenHands, 0.835 F1), confirming that it captures general progress signals.\n604\tDeployed as an early-termination trigger, ProgressProbe saves 32.7\\% of tokens while preserving 96.1\\% of task success---a practical path to more efficient agent execution.\n605\t\n606\t% ============================================================\n607\t% REFERENCES\n608\t% ============================================================\n609\t\\begin{thebibliography}{20}\n610\t\n611\t\\bibitem[Yang et al.(2024)]{yang2024swe}\n612\tJohn Yang, Carlos E.\\ Jimenez, Alexander Wettig, Kilian Lieret, Shunyu Yao, Karthik Narasimhan, and Ofir Press.\n613\t\\newblock SWE-agent: Agent-computer interfaces enable automated software engineering.\n614\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2405.15793}, 2024.\n615\t\n616\t\\bibitem[Wang et al.(2024)]{wang2024opendevin}\n617\tXingyao Wang, Boxuan Li, Yufan Song, Frank F.\\ Xu, Xiangru Tang, Mingchen Zhuge, Jiayi Pan, Yueqi Song, Bowen Li, Jaskirat Singh, et al.\n618\t\\newblock OpenDevin: An open platform for AI software developers as generalist agents.\n619\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2407.16741}, 2024.\n620\t\n621\t\\bibitem[Chen et al.(2024a)]{chen2024codeagent}\n622\tYangruibo Chen, Marcus J.\\ Min, Saurabh Sinha, and Baishakhi Ray.\n623\t\\newblock On the effectiveness of code agent monitoring and self-repair.\n624\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint}, 2024.\n625\t\n626\t\\bibitem[Guo et al.(2024)]{guo2024specra}\n627\tZhixuan Guo, Changzhi Sun, and Wanxiang Che.\n628\t\\newblock SpecRA: Speculation with early rejection for efficient LLM agent interactions.\n629\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint}, 2024.\n630\t\n631\t\\bibitem[Luo et al.(2024)]{luo2024agentprm}\n632\tZhiyu Luo, Wei Shen, and others.\n633\t\\newblock AgentPRM: Process reward models for LLM agent planning.\n634\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint}, 2024.\n635\t\n636\t\\bibitem[Chen et al.(2024b)]{chen2024swetrace}\n637\tYuntao Chen and others.\n638\t\\newblock SWE-TRACE: Trajectory-level credit assignment for software engineering agents.\n639\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint}, 2024.\n640\t\n641\t\\bibitem[Zhang et al.(2024)]{zhang2024agentcollab}\n642\tShishir Zhang, Jun Araki, and others.\n643\t\\newblock Agent collaboration for self-reflection and planning.\n644\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint}, 2024.\n645\t\n646\t\\bibitem[Ratner et al.(2017)]{ratner2017snorkel}\n647\tAlexander Ratner, Stephen H.\\ Bach, Henry Ehrenberg, Jason Fries, Sen Wu, and Christopher R{\\'e}.\n648\t\\newblock Snorkel: Rapid training data creation with weak supervision.\n649\t\\newblock In \\emph{Proceedings of the VLDB Endowment}, 11(3):269--282, 2017.\n650\t\n651\t\\bibitem[Ratner et al.(2019)]{ratner2019weaksuper}\n652\tAlexander Ratner, Braden Hancock, Jared Dunnmon, Frederic Sala, Shreyash Pandey, and Christopher R{\\'e}.\n653\t\\newblock Training complex models with multi-task weak supervision.\n654\t\\newblock In \\emph{Proceedings of AAAI}, 2019.\n655\t\n656\t\\bibitem[Belinkov(2022)]{belinkov2022probing}\n657\tYonatan Belinkov.\n658\t\\newblock Probing classifiers: Promises, shortcomings, and advances.\n659\t\\newblock \\emph{Computational Linguistics}, 48(1):207--219, 2022.\n660\t\n661\t\\end{thebibliography}\n662\t\n663\t\\end{document}\n664\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01MbVMY3G7diYAUeDjF1aRAe","session_id":"3eaa2ba6-aa45-4be4-a541-eab7cb4981f6","uuid":"014023b5-0784-4f2f-9e74-1250a7e407aa","timestamp":"2026-05-14T08:59:48.680Z"}
